{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The reduction is undermined by an equivocation on 'internal consistency'. SRC_003's sensitivity analysis (perturbing inputs/checklist items) measures *causal robustness* — whether the panel's judgment is causally driven by task-relevant reasoning rather than coincidental alignment with the answer key. SRC_001's 'internal consistency in checklist execution' measures *procedural coherence* — whether the panel's items are mutually non-contradictory and executed in order. The refutation condition (correct under perturbation + low internal consistency) is only fatal if the two are identical; but a panel can have low procedural coherence (skip/reorder items) yet high causal robustness (judgment still tracks the perturbed evidence), making the reduction false rather than refuted.","counterexample":[{"panel_behavior":"skips reordered item, still tracks perturbed evidence correctly","perturbation":"swap two checklist items that are causally relevant but procedurally equivalent","reduction_status":"the reduction falsely labels this as 'low internal consistency' while sensitivity analysis confirms genuine reliability, so the reduction does not hold","src001_internal_consistency":"low (procedural violation)","src003_sensitivity_result":"judgment changes with perturbation — genuine reliability"},{"panel_behavior":"resolves conflict correctly using domain logic","perturbation":"introduce a conflicting item","reduction_status":"reduction fails because internal-consistency-as-defined-by-SRC001 is ambiguous between procedural and causal senses","src001_internal_consistency":"low (conflicting items present)","src003_sensitivity_result":"judgment stable — genuine reliability"}],"target":"084ee737e7b2dda646fc2bd6cfceabd1eaee629549defde8d58241f8c4f1d0f3"},{"attack":true,"case":"The decoy-flagging mechanism is a test of *vigilance*, not of *reliability for objectively verifiable outcomes*. The problem asks for evidence distinguishing reliability gain from compliance *for tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes*, but a panel can reliably produce the correct verifiable outcome (e.g., via the non-decoy items) yet fail to flag the decoy because it has no contradiction with the verifiable answer. The counterexample shows a panel that is causally tracking the verifiable outcome (correct under perturbation of substantive items) but treats the decoy as harmless background noise — it neither checks it off (compliance) nor flags it (vigilance). SRC_002's binary (compliant=check it / reliable=flag it) thus misclassifies a reliable panel as neither, breaking the evidence-distinguishing claim.","counterexample":[{"actual_reliability":"high (judgment tracks perturbations of substantive items)","classification_failure":"SRC_002's evidence criterion produces an indeterminate verdict for a genuinely reliable panel","decoy_item":"'Confirm the widget factory has adequate parking' (logically irrelevant to defect)","panel_behavior":"ignores decoy entirely (neither checks nor flags), yet still tracks substantive perturbations correctly","src002_prediction":"neither 'compliant' (didn't check decoy) nor 'reliable' (didn't flag decoy)","verifiable_outcome":"correctly identifies the defective widget under perturbation of its color/weight specs"}],"target":"2da4907b0ca06e729e82db54693f348b1e8eef412466d95f5b25f052221676fd"},{"attack":true,"case":"The 'expert is a compressed panel' claim is refuted by tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes where intuition is not an internalized checklist but a recognition-based shortcut that *systematically diverges* from the checklist on a recognizable subset of cases. The expert's non-linear pattern recognition can be reliable on average yet wrong on the perturbation cases that distinguish reliability from compliance, whereas the panel's externalized reasoning catches them. The counterexample shows a task where expert and panel agree on average but the expert's 'compressed checklist' is actually a different (recognitional) algorithm that fails exactly where the audit trail would have caught the error — meaning the expert's reliability is not the panel's reliability plus compression, but a different process that is less reliable on the distinguishable subset.","counterexample":[{"expert":"uses gestalt visual recognition — correct on average, but systematically misses subtle presentations of a rare subtype","outcome":"expert and panel agree on ~85% of cases, so 'both reach the same verifiable outcome in most cases' holds, yet the expert's failures are concentrated where the checklist succeeds, so the reliability difference is not 'audit trail availability' but a difference in the underlying algorithm","panel":"using the checklist flags the subtype's features, correct on the subtype","task":"radiology screening with a gold-standard biopsy outcome"}],"target":"67ea8940376144137568677022dc8daf1a55572f8e470cd09edf426a33b982c6"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004's evidence criterion is non-discriminating for the population of real checklists. A well-designed explicit checklist for tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes is typically *intentionally consistent* (it is engineered to be), so by SRC_004's own countercondition the test has no errors to detect and yields no evidence in the common case. More importantly, a merely-compliant panel and a reliable panel produce the *same observable behavior* on a consistent checklist (both execute the items and reach the verifiable outcome); the distinguishing evidence SRC_004 needs (flagging a runtime error) is absent because there is no error to flag. Thus for the large class of consistent checklists, SRC_004 provides no way to distinguish compliance from reliability, leaving the problem's central question unanswered for those tasks.","counterexample":[{"checklist":"a consistent, well-engineered defect-detection checklist with no internal contradictions","compliant_panel":"executes every item literally, reaches correct outcome, never flags anything","observable_difference":"none — both produce identical checklist traces and the same correct outcome","problem_requirement":"distinguish genuine reliability from mere compliance","reliable_panel":"executes every item, understands why each matters, reaches correct outcome, has nothing to flag","result":"SRC_004 fails to distinguish for any task using a consistent checklist","src004_verdict":"indeterminate (no runtime error to detect)"}],"target":"75f8800084ff538c2d8d93ac9e4d31834a734032de748a5c7f376a3a7b57a060"}]}